   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend chapter 617 of the laws of 1937 relating to an additional\nalternative form of government for the county of Westchester, in relation to\nthe authority of the state civil service commission to administer the county\nservice in Westchester",
    "basePrintNo": "S10572",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S10572-2025",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §§92 & 94, Chap 617 of 1937",
    "lawSection": "Westchester County",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S10572\n\nSPONSOR: STEWART-COUSINS\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend chapter 617 of the laws of 1937 relating to an addi-\ntional alternative form of government for the county of Westchester, in\nrelation to the authority of the state civil service commission to\nadminister the county service in Westchester\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nThe purpose of this bill is to align the process for changing jurisdic-\ntional classification positions in Westchester County with the process\nfound in agencies across the State.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 of the bill amends the laws of 1937 to remove the State Civil\nService Commission's ability to act in personnel areas, including the\njurisdictional classification of positions in Westchester County.\n\nSection 2 amends the laws of 1937 to remove the requirement that the\nState Civil Service Commission approve any procedure involving appoint-\nments, separations, promotions, demotions. transfers, or reinstatements\nof positions in Westchester County.\n\nSection 3 is the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nCivil Service Law requires that all titles are initially classified in\nthe Competitive class. Under certain circumstances defined in the Law, a\ntitle may be reclassified out of the Competitive class and placed into\none of four other jurisdictional classifications: Labor, Non-Competi-\ntive, Exempt, or Unclassified.\n\nExisting law establishes a specific process for making such changes,\nincluding notice, public hearing and submission of a request to the New\nYork State Civil Service Commission. For most agencies across New York\nState, once the Commission approves the request, the reclassification\nchange is filed with the Secretary of State and takes effect. However,\nChapter 617 of the laws of 1937 treats Westchester County differently.\nFor Westchester County, Commission approval is not sufficient. A sepa-\nrate, explicit signatory approval from the Governor is required before\nany change can be implemented.\n\nAs a result, Westchester County has experienced significant delays in\ntitle reclassification, often extending the process for months. The\nimpact is particularly acute for NY HELPS (Hiring for Emergency Limited\nPlacement) appointments, where some approvals have been pending for\nnearly a year. These delays adversely affect employees who must wait for\nappointments to be finalized. This bill would remove certain powers of\nthe state civil service commission regarding jurisdictional classifica-\ntion positions, aligning Westchester County's process with the rest of\nthe State.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nThis is a new bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nTo be determined.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
